so here's a question why am I talking  about education we have to look at how  we're setting up the next generation are  we doing enough to support young people  to thrive one of the things that I  personally struggled with when I went to  school is there was only one way to  judge a student which is how you  performed in your classes and I was  never the kind of person who was  motivated by grades I learned things at  school that weren't recognized or  rewarded I have a DD and so I was  terrible at paying attention terrible at  reading I'm really bad at reading so I  had to learn to ask good questions I had  to learn to go after class to talk to  the teacher I had to learn to go up to  my friends and say can you explain that  to me I became a good question ask her  and I became a really good listener the  solutions I found to the challenge is of  my own childhood became my strength as  an adult so are we doing that at school  now are we helping kids find solutions  to their own challenges which likely  will become their strengths as adults  when we talk about the what of Education  we're gonna talk about grades and  classrooms and we're gonna talk about  stuff that we generally talk about now  but if we talk about the why of  Education we're gonna ask an entirely  different questions we're gonna ask how  we can help children be more curious  we're gonna ask how we can help them  learn empathy we're gonna ask and try  and figure out how we can help foster  their passions rather than just help  them make a grade  so many people make a big point of the  number of jobs that exist today that  couldn't even conceive of 20 or 30 years  ago so of course the same will be 20 or  30 years from now and people will always  have to play a role there's gonna be a  lot of redefining what work looks like I  for one do not buy into the whole things  that AI and robotics is gonna replace  everything we like human interaction and  I think the education system can play a  much bigger role in preparing us to be  human we measure a student's performance  how well they perform on a test but we  have no metrics in school for the whole  person and we ignore their ability to  lead we ignore their ability to  communicate so I think when we teach  kids how to come together how to  organize how to have opinions I think  that that's what our future will hold  they will do the things we teach them  exams at school of course are important  but they're not all important we put so  much pressure on kids to make the grade  we treat education like a finite game  like school is the only thing but  education is our whole lives so how do  we use school to prepare a child to  continue to learn for their entire life  we want to be completely open to the  idea that we have more to learn I don't  think anybody goes to teaching college  and dreams of becoming a teacher because  they just want to teach math there's a  service component to becoming a teacher  that there's an element of giving back  to do something of higher purpose it has  nothing to do with the subjects they  teach but what is the underlying cause  that would inspire them to become a  teacher in the first place  administration's have to obsess with  creating an environment in which  teachers want to come to school in which  they feel that the administration has  their backs if I ask any school  administrator what's your number one  priority they're gonna tell me the  student it's completely wrong the  responsibility of leadership has nothing  to do with the results leaders are  responsible for the people who are  responsible for the results  administration serves the teachers and  teachers serve the students and when the  administration believes that the numbers  are everything the teachers will protect  themselves from their own leaders and  then the students will suffer  the things that I think are so good  about Generation Z is how open-minded  they are how accepting they are they  really are driven by inclusion and  acceptance I think that's a huge benefit  to the future of society I love that  they're an activist generation they're  not just hashtagging their discontent  their arranging rallies and they're  actually showing up  they're coming of age right now and  they're coming of age in a time of  stress in the world they're seeing  tension in their own countries  political divides that we have never  seen before and this is forming their  worldview I think some of the  liabilities that their generation has to  face and has to deal with is that we are  seeing increasing rates of addiction to  social media and suicide depression  anxiety and we're letting young people  grow up and just hoping that it works  out but why not teach them why not guide  how to interact with other human beings  what's appropriate and what's  inappropriate we can take responsibility  for teaching some of these skills I  think one of the most difficult things  about reforming the education system is  admitting that we have accountability if  we imagine a world that's different to  the one we live in now we all have to  take some responsibility that the future  leaders are going to come from the  schools that we have now and if we  better equip students now for what the  future will look like our world will be  better it's not just about the teachers  and the students every single one of us  has to play a role every single one of  us has to take responsibility for each  other  at every age especially kids were going  to school now  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you  [Applause]
 

